the last $m$m values is one of $m+1$m+1 possibilities, and there are at most $(m+1)^m$(m+1)m distinct states.\n\nA computation with finitely many states, stepping deterministically, must revisit a state. From the moment it does, everything afterwards repeats. So a period exists, and the argument even bounds how long it can take to appear.\n\nThat bound is enormous and nearly useless in practice — for a set with largest move 6 it allows some 117,000 states before repetition — and the actual periods are tiny. What the argument gives is not the number but the guarantee: searching for a period in a subtraction game is a search for something known to be there.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsubtraction game — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/subtraction%20game)\nciteturn5search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; A result of Golomb~(1966) shows that every subtraction game with a finite move set has an eventually periodic nim-sequence, but the known proof yields only an exponential upper bound on the period length. ... Keywords: Additive Subtraction Game, Nimber, Periodicity, Sink Convention.\nFor $a\\ge2$ and $c\\ne a+b$, we give three explicit sufficient tests for pure periodicity, associated with the candidate periods $a+b$, $c+a$, and $c+b$. For fixed $a,b$, the tests depend only on $c\\bmod(a+b)$ and are computed from the losing positions of the two-move game $\\{a,b\\}$. Whenever a test succeeds, we give the losing positions in closed form, reconstruct the remaining values, and prove that the least period is the smallest candidate whose test succeeds. The construction shows how the added move $c$ modifies the two-move pattern to form a repeating block. We also give a uniform formulation of the known additive case $c=a+b$. For $a\\ge2$, we conjecture that the tests cover every purely periodic non-additive game. In this range, when $c\\ge2(a+b)$, we prove necessity for purely periodic games with least period at most $a+b$.\n\nsequence subtraction game\n\n2026-08-18\n\nKai Liang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nInfinite Subtraction Games with Periodic Outcomes and Aperiodic SG Values\n\nWe find some significant special cases of subtraction games with infinite subtraction sets, whose outcome sequences are periodic, but whose SG value sequences are bounded and aperiodic.\n\nsequence subtraction game\n\n2026-01-26 v2\n\nAnjali Bhagat, Urban Larsson, Hikaru Manabe, Takahiro Yamashita\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nAdditive sink subtraction\n\nSubtraction games are a classical topic in Combinatorial Game Theory. A result of Golomb~(1966) shows that every subtraction game with a finite move set has an eventually periodic nim-sequence, but the known proof yields only an exponential upper bound on the period length. Flammenkamp (1997) conjectures a striking classification for three-move subtraction games: non-additive rulesets exhibit linear period lengths of the form ``the sum of two moves'', where the choice of which two moves displays fractal-like behavior, while additive sets $S=\\{a,b,a+b\\}$ have purely periodic outcomes with linear or quadratic period lengths. Despite early attention in \\emph{Winning Ways} (1982), the general additive case remains open. We introduce and analyze a dual winning convention, which we call {\\sc sink subtraction}. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Finite subtraction games in more than one dimension (https://www.academia.edu/125535179/Finite_subtraction_games_in_more_than_one_dimension?force_claim_to_highlight=true)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; In [45] Siegel studies subtraction games where every removal but a finite set is allowed and proves the arithmetic periodicity of nim-values.5 Researchers have been interested in the question of whether there is any subtraction game with an aperiodic but bounded nim-sequence. ... Theorem 7 (Row-Column Eventual Periodicity). ... (Their framework generalizes both misère and normal-play.)\n The outcomes of any instance of one-dimensional finite subtraction are ultimately periodic [28]. We prove that such results still hold in two dimensions when we restrict the view to rows or columns: twodimensional finite subtraction is eventually row and column periodic. In contrast, the general problem of ‘periodicity’ for finite vector subtraction is much harder, and indeed, a recent result [37, 41] proves Turing completeness of a slight extension of such games. In a follow up [32], Gurvich et al. show that finite vector subtraction is EXP-complete. P Instances of infinite vector subtraction are well-studied. Popular examples are nim (S = {as | i si = 1, a ∈ N}) and wythoff nim [48] (S = {(a, 0), (0, a), (a, a) | a ∈ N}) and their many variations; see for example [15] for a survey on a multitude of variations of wythoff nim. nim has become famous because of its generic capacity in encoding any impartial normal play game, via the famous Sprague-Grundy theory. In contrast, not many papers study finite vector subtraction. This ruleset has a quadratic polynomial period length in the parameter a and no preperiod. They consider four-move of the form S = {a, 4a, 12a + 1, 16a + 1} with 1 ⩽ a ⩽ 26, and prove pure periodicity; the period length is a cubic polynomial in the parameter a. They wonder if the five-move ruleset S = {a, 8a, 30a + 1, 37a + 1, 38a + 1}, a ∈ N, has super-polynomial period length. Flammenkamp [20] expands on [3]. He searches for long periods in finite subtraction via extensive computations. He shows that three-move and other rulsets, given max S = s3 follow various fractal-looking behaviors on the classification of preperiods and periods, whether they are of the form s1 +s2 , s1 +s3 , s2 +s3 , or something else. He shows that very few have other period lengths, and that about half of the rulesets have period length s1 + s3 . Flammenkamp is not convinced by the mentioned five-move problem from [3]. He suggests instead various four-move and five-move rulesets, where he, through extensive computations, provides heuristics that support “exponential” rather than “polynomial” eventual period lengths, in some cases via a ruleset parameter or in other cases via a “record holder” ruleset in terms of max S. The conjecture is stated in two different regimes. When S is additive, i.e. c = a + b, the period is at most a quadratic polynomial in a and b. This part is stated as a theorem in [20], but concerning only outcomes, both [4] and [6] have at least partial solutions to this part. Both [20] and [46] conjecture that when c ̸= a + b, the period length has seven possibilities; in essence the period length is a divisor of the sum of two elements of the subtraction set. Coleman et al. [12] consider two-move subtraction. They describe necessary and sufficient conditions for obtaining a given nimvalue at a position. Zhang [50] studies how the period of the nim-sequence changes when one adjoins a move to a given ruleset. The subtraction game literature and periodicity-related questions expand to partizan subtraction games (where the players have different subtraction sets) [24]. Duchene et al. [16] show that computing the preperiod length of the outcome sequence is NP-hard. They compute the period length when each player’s subtraction set is of size at most two. As mentioned, finite subtraction games have eventually periodic nim-sequences. What can one say about infinite subtraction games? Of course, classical nim is a simple example with an aperiodic and unbounded nim-sequence. In [45] Siegel studies subtraction games where every removal but a finite set is allowed and proves the arithmetic periodicity of nim-values.5 Researchers have been interested in the question of whether there is any subtraction game with an aperiodic but bounded nim-sequence. Fox [21] found a positive answer, using a technique from combinatorics on words, via a ruleset with an aperiodic nim-sequence bounded by 3, but the description of the subtraction set and the proofs are complicated. Inspired by this, Fox and Larsson [22] provides a simpler game and analysis with the same properties. They consider the subtraction set S = {F2n+1 − 1}n∈N = {1, 4, 12, · · · } where Fk is the kth Fibonacci number with F0 = 0, F1 = 1. 2.2. Other settings. Cohensius et al. [11] solve instances of scoring-play subtraction games, referred to as cumulative subtraction.  In practice, row periodicity is very rare. Definition 6 (Row Eventual Periodicity). Consider a ruleset S in two-dimensional subtraction. ′ a) Consider row j ∈ N0 . The outcomes are row j eventually periodic if there exists a column i ∈ N0 and a T > 0 such that ∀i ⩾ i′ o(i, j) = o(i + T, j). b) The smallest such i′ ∈ N0 is the preperiod length of row j. c) The outcomes are row eventually periodic if ∀j ⩾ 0 the outcomes are row j eventually periodic. Theorem 7 (Row-Column Eventual Periodicity). Consider two-dimensional finite subtraction. The outcomes are row (column) eventually periodic. Proof. Let S = {(ai , bi )}1⩽i⩽k be the subtraction set, and let A = maxi {ai }. Fix a row y, and define the window W (x, y) = (o(x, y), o(x + 1, y), · · · , o(x + A − 1, y)). The outcome function o(x, y) ∈ {N , P}. Hence there are at most 2A possible outcome patterns within the window range. We prove the theorem using induction. Base case: the outcomes of row 0 are eventually row periodic. We first prove this statement. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGrundy sequences, and where they stop being predictable · Combinatorial Game Theory (https://www.combinatorial-game-theory.com/essays/grundy-sequences/)\nciteturn5search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; ## The one theorem there is #\n\nFor finite subtraction sets, the answer is known and it is good news. ... It gives a bound on when periodicity starts and how long the period is — but the bound is $(|S|+1)^m$(∣S∣+1)m, which for a set with largest element twenty is astronomically larger than any period ever observed.\n## The one theorem there is #\n\nFor finite subtraction sets, the answer is known and it is good news.\n\nIf $S$S is finite with largest element $m$m, the Grundy sequence is eventually periodic. The proof is a counting argument: the Grundy value of a heap depends only on the previous $m$m values, and no Grundy value can exceed $|S|$∣S∣, so there are at most $(|S|+1)^m$(∣S∣+1)m possible windows of $m$m consecutive values. The sequence of windows must eventually repeat, and once a window repeats the entire subsequent sequence repeats.\n\nThat is a pigeonhole argument, and it is worth noticing what it does and does not give. It gives eventual periodicity, guaranteed. It gives a bound on when periodicity starts and how long the period is — but the bound is $(|S|+1)^m$(∣S∣+1)m, which for a set with largest element twenty is astronomically larger than any period ever observed. The theorem says the sequence repeats and is useless for predicting when.\n\nEvery actual period is found by computing until it appears.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLessons in Play: An Introduction to Combinatorial Game Theory | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227859255_Lessons_in_Play_An_Introduction_to_Combinatorial_Game_Theory)\nciteturn5search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; For example, it is well known (see [14], Chapter 4, Theorem 2.4 and 2.5) that all finite subtraction games are ultimately periodic, and a periodicity test has been shown in [1] (see Chapter 7, Corollary 7.34). ... In [1], the game 0.205200C is said to be sappregular, which means that the G-sequence is an interlacing of two periodic subsequences with an arithmetic-periodic one; this behavior occurs also in variants of octal games with pass moves [12].\n... For example, it is w